# Copy relevant lines into backend/event_bus/.env (or your process manager / Elastic Beanstalk / ECS task def).
#
# --- ElastiCache (AWS) ---
# 1) Create a Redis cluster in the same VPC as the event_bus / API instances (e.g. serverless or cache.t3 micro for dev).
# 2) Security group: allow TCP 6379 (or 6380) from the security group used by your API/ALB instances.
# 3) Use TLS if your cluster requires it (ElastiCache in-transit encryption):
#    rediss://:YOUR_AUTH_TOKEN@primary.xxxxxx.0001.use1.cache.amazonaws.com:6379/0
#    (rediss = TLS; add ?ssl_cert_reqs=none if you use self-signed / doc your team’s policy)
# 4) Point every read-serving API node at the same URL so the shared read cache is consistent.
#
# Optional: single local Redis
# REDIS_CACHE_URL=redis://127.0.0.1:6379/0
#
# REQUIRED to enable distributed read cache (unset = in-process only, per instance)
# REDIS_CACHE_URL=
#
# Set 1 to force in-process cache only (e.g. local dev)
# REDIS_CACHE_DISABLE=0
#
# If Redis is down, keep serving with per-node memory cache (default 1)
# REDIS_CACHE_FALLBACK=1
#
# Key prefix to avoid collisions on a shared Redis
# REDIS_CACHE_KEY_PREFIX=polaris:event_bus:read:
#
# Long TTL for "explicit" metadata keys (safety; still invalidated on writes)
# REDIS_EXPLICIT_TTL_SECONDS=604800
